body:
As @littledan noted, there could be a privacy risk in firing this global event -- if the event is fired simultaneously in different tabs or in different browsing contexts, then it becomes an additional cross-origin signal for identifying and tracking a user.

That risk has been noted in previous APIs (I came up with: Idle, Ambient Light, Proximity, Generic Sensor and MediaCapture between 2012 and 2016) with different mitigations noted in each. In particular, if events are fired for the visible, active tab (and fired for other tabs when they become active), that would reduce the usability for tracking. And in some cases, specs have noted that fuzzing in time is possible (if background and embedded parties have useful reasons to find out about the change, but functionality doesn't rely on precisely identical timing).

@domenic I haven't looked through all of HTML for other events, but at a glance it does seem like `languagechange` and `offline`/`online` could have similar effects.

A test would look something like this:
```js
async_test(t => {
  const beforeChange = currentJSTimeZone();
  ontimezonechange = () => {
    assert_not_equals(currentJSTimeZone(), beforeChange);
    t.done();
  }
  changeOSTimeZone(); // privileged call that would need harness support
  assert_equals(currentJSTimeZone(), beforeChange);
})

> It is. We have similar functions to create OS events such as keyboard or mouse input. It's the same kind of thing.

Look at your propose test again. I believe the line "assert_equals(currentJSTimeZone(), beforeChange);" is unrelated to the scope of "Add a timezonechange event to Window/WorkerGlobalScop".

Think about this way. IF we have the following code but without adding/implementing the timezonechange event. Should the following code ALSO pass, from your point of view?

```
async_test(t => {
  const beforeChange = currentJSTimeZone();
  changeOSTimeZone(); // privileged call that would need harness support
  assert_equals(currentJSTimeZone(), beforeChange);
})
```

If your answer is YES, then it prove that line is non-unique between having this event or not because the 2 line js code in the eventhandler itself neither change the value of beforeChange nor change what the currentJSTimeZone() would return. What currentJSTimeZone would return is not depending on the JavaScript ontimezonechange event, but depending on OS level timezonechange event, which is there regardless we add  the  timezonechange event to JavaScript or not.

I am not saying that assertion line should be true or false. I am saying that line is not testing *the effect of the JavaScript timezonechange event* and therefore irrelevant.

Given the above, I've asked Mozilla to revisit their position on this proposal (which is currently "defer"): https://github.com/mozilla/standards-positions/issues/241

@littledan Do you want to amend the patch to explicitly call out that UAs should consider mitigation strategies such as delaying the event for inactive execution contexts?

Other than all that, I guess we still need some WPT tests + harness support for the special `changeOSTimeZone` hook. Who wants to take that on?

@asankah 

> My reading of the text is that it doesn't preclude a UA from implementing mitigation strategies involving the presentation of the new TZ to JS execution contexts at different points in time.

I agree that the text does not preclude a UA from implementing mitigations to the concerns from @npdoty [noted above](https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/3047#issuecomment-573847868). However, @tomlowenthal [argued above](https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/3047#issuecomment-574833920) that specific mitigation strategies should be recommended in the specification.

@asankah do you see disadvantages to recommending a specific mitigation?

@annevk @litherum Do other engines agree with landing this with privacy mitigations left up to the UA? Or should we put the recommendation of delay discussed until the tab is focused into the spec?

@littledan, @annevk  
Seconded. I'm in favor of only firing the event and exposing the changed timezone to the focused browsing context as mentioned by @npdoty and @tomlowenthal  above.

Relatedly, is there a formalized concept for the active document of a top-level browsing context that has system input focus?

@annevk I didn't address your question at the end of your comment.

The top-level site based isolation method assumes that documents can share information with documents in their nested documents, but not across non-same-site top-level documents and their nested documents. ([Described here](https://github.com/asankah/identity-domains)).

These boundaries of course aren't quite there yet. Restrictions on third-party cookies and other proposed mitigations including storage isolation intend to address that.
